our car credit Christa Bauer is in wolfs  Borges Car Museum  looking for an interesting car to try  out of the 200 automotive milestones on  display his attention is drawn to a  revolutionary design that flopped on the  market  and as Hugh's ROA T introduced in 1967  the futuristic sedan featured a number  of groundbreaking innovations it was the  first German automobile to win Europe's  Car of the Year award Cristo says NSU  which until then had only made small  cars built the ROA T as a full-fledged  attempt at an upmarket midsize car it  had a rotary engine a futuristic design  front-wheel drive fully independent  suspension and disc brakes on each wheel  all the ingredients for success  it seemed ns used marketing campaign  invited comparison with the established  players here a proud new owner tells us  friendly car has amazing aerodynamics  NSU gambled that the rotary engine and  advanced aerodynamics would be enough to  challenge Mercedes and BMW the  advantages of the rotary piston engine  are that it produces very little  vibration and has good revving power  unfortunately the engine also had  chronic problems many owners had to  replace engines after less than 20,000  kilometers Christoph says people used to  joke that drivers had a special greeting  to show how many engines their car had  already gone through and in fact the  engine did initially have bugs NSU had  to replace so many for free that the  company's accountants were in shock but  by 1970 the engine problems had been  solved this car from 1977  still purrs sweetly with its original  engine  rotary engines get their power from high  engine speed instead of engine size the  RO atntion displaced just one liter but  produced 115 horsepower and had a top  speed of 180 kilometers an hour this 45  year old car feels almost as civilized  as a modern car well almost  it's destined off to puff T a loud bang  from the exhaust leads christophe to  remarked that this car seems to think  it's a rocket he says that although it's  not especially sporty it is a great  looking car features like the low wedge  like shape and sharply raked windshield  which is great for the aerodynamics  catch the eye but the most distinctive  touch for Christoph is this crease that  goes all the way around the car and  gives it a tidy elegance that tidiness  ends under the hood however we're a nest  of pipes and hoses hides the little  rotary engine does it stuff that can't  even claim or track the smooth body  shape gave the car a drag coefficient of  0.35 which was sensational at the time  and better than that of a modern day out  er8 sports car  the interior is straightforward and  unpretentious an automatic transmission  power steering and the big cushy seats  make it a comfortable car for longer  trips  the engine is mounted low which gives  the car a low center of gravity that  makes the aerodynamic shape possible but  there's one other problem rotary engines  in general are not very fuel-efficient  and tooling around these country roads  in Tuscany the roa t burns up 17 litres  of gasoline for every 100 kilometres the  transmission doesn't help  the RO ad has a semi-automatic  transmission there's no clutch pedal so  the driver just moves the gearshift to  change gears christophe says that takes  some getting used to but it works fine  the only problem is the automatic shift  slowly and there's not much torque so  even with 115 horsepower it's not a very  sporty car rock solid v tempest  michelada an aspartic angling that  accelerating from 0 to 100 takes about  14 seconds which wasn't bad for a sedan  in the late 60s but the rotary engines  performance makes driving the car a  fairly unexcited endeavor  instead the roa t feels sedate and  distinguished  Evelyn Sunita Hamza it's not easy being  a revolutionary Christophe says that  while the ROA T was a failure at the  time its futuristic design and the  rotary engine make it one of the most  advanced cars of the 60s for our tester 